{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a notable Crusader lord who lived during the 12th century. He belonged to the House of Toulouse and ruled as the Count of Tripoli from around 1152 until his death in 1187. Raymond was the second son of Pons Count of Tripoli and Cecilia of France the daughter of King Louis VI of France. His brother Raymond II died without heirs making Raymond the eventual successor to the Tripolitanian throne.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early life and accession**\nRaymond III spent his early years in France where his family had significant ties. His upbringing likely included exposure to the cultural and political influences of both the French royal court and the Crusader states in the Levant. When his brother Raymond II passed away without leaving any legitimate heirs Raymond inherited the title of Count of Tripoli in 1152. His accession marked the beginning of a period of political maneuvering in the region.\n\nUpon taking control Raymond III faced a variety of challenges. The most pressing issue was the unstable political landscape within the Crusader states. The nearby Principality of Antioch and the Kingdom of Jerusalem were frequently embroiled in conflicts both internal and external. Raymond tried to balance the interests of his county with those of his powerful neighbors while navigating complex alliances and rivalries. He found himself caught in the tensions between Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus and the Western Crusader states on one hand and the Muslim powers led by Nur ad-Din and later Saladin on the other.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Marriage and alliances**\nIn 1153 Raymond III married Hodierna of Jericho the daughter of Joscelin II Count of Edessa. This marriage was strategic as it linked the House of Tripoli with another prominent Crusader family. The union produced several children including Raymond IV and Bohemond IV who inherited his titles and continued his legacy. Hodierna played an active role in the political affairs of Tripoli often accompanying her husband on military campaigns and diplomatic missions.\n\nOne significant alliance Raymond forged was with the Byzantine Empire. In 1164 he married his daughter Maria off to Manuel I Comnenus’s nephew Renier of Montferrat. This was part of a broader effort by Raymond to strengthen ties with the Byzantines and possibly secure their support against the advancing Muslim forces. The alliance however proved to be short-lived and complicated by shifting political winds.\n\nRaymond’s diplomatic efforts were not limited to the marriage alliances. He worked to maintain peace within the Crusader states often mediating between factions. In 1164 he hosted a meeting between Baldwin III of Jerusalem and Manuel I Comnenus in his court at Tripoli. This meeting aimed to consolidate the Crusader states against the growing threat from Nur ad-Din but ultimately ended without significant agreements.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Later life military actions and death**\nIn the latter years of his life Raymond III became more directly involved in military conflicts. The rise of Saladin as the leader of the Muslim world posed a serious threat to the Crusader states. Raymond participated in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where Baldwin IV of Jerusalem achieved a notable victory against Saladin’s forces.\n\nRaymond remained active in the political affairs of the region even as his health declined. He attended the Third Crusade led by Frederick Barbarossa in 1189 though he did not live to see its conclusion. Frederick’s army crossed through the territory of Tripoli but Raymond died in 1187 before Frederick arrived. His death left a power vacuum in Tripoli which his son Bohemond IV inherited.\n\nRaymond III’s legacy lies in his role as a stabilizer in an unstable region. His diplomatic skills and alliances were crucial 